t $y={2}^{x-2}+3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y={2}^{x}.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y={2}^{x-2}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y={2}^{x-2}+3.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+2,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b+3).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y={2}^{x}$, we have that ${2}^{a}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y={2}^{x-2}+3.$ Substituting $x=a+2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={2}^{a+2-2}+3 \\\\  & ={2}^{a}+3 \\\\  & =b+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a+2,b+3)$ must lie on $y={2}^{x-2}+3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059669 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38b450bd-b43a-507c-9bbe-0aaf7a64a3ed" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve Given Another Transformed Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30f927d8-7469-5f46-bcd8-ceb6220bf583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254097} {:answer/id #uuid "5679b0cb-b917-5834-9cfc-21236c05bf34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254431} {:answer/id #uuid "8ddf4522-8bdc-5052-93ac-81ce5d766651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254921} {:answer/id #uuid "d96d1372-c430-5db2-a838-5534030331cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255597} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8c348e-dc48-52a8-a7a2-dcc717c081b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30f927d8-7469-5f46-bcd8-ceb6220bf583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254097} :answer-field/id #uuid "65ce4959-7406-5c8f-b9dc-e08ef35e4173" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e35c2dec-10c0-57fb-accb-533118e28579" :question/math-academy-id "q-121414" :question/problem "The point  $(4,8)$ lies on the curve $y=4f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+3?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(4,8)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+3:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=4f(x).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8)=(4,2).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,2)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(4,-(2))=(4,-2).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+3.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-2)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(4,-2+3)=(4,1).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(4,8)$ lies on the graph of $y=4f(x)$, we have that \n\n$$\n4f(4)=8\\,⟹\\,f(4)=2.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+3.$ Substituting $x=4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(4)+3 \\\\  & =-2+3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(4,1)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+3.$"}]}
